From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 21:38:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09311/11362] Record that the scored induce budget cannot be
 spent inside the scored induce timeout

The kernel sets CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_MAX_TOKENS=131072 and CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TIMEOUT=2400 by
env, and both are honoured by the live construction sites, so spending the full budget
needs 54.6 tok/s. Measured on the scored Blackwell card today: 52.2 single-stream on
llama.cpp, 56.1 single-stream on vLLM, and 45.2, 30.2, 20.4 per stream at k=4, k=16, k=32.
The ceiling is unreachable at every measured concurrency and the gap widens as concurrency
rises -- intrinsically, since aggregate throughput is bought by lowering per-stream rate
while the timeout is per-call. The backend shipped today therefore widens it.

Not claimed: that it ever fires. That depends on real induce lengths, which are not
measured. The A/B's 81,812-token generation is suggestive but ran under a different budget
and envelope.

Recorded because the failure is silent in the bad direction: the code already warns that a
fired induce timeout makes generate() fail and the agent run LLM-OFF, so a long induction
yields no generator rather than a worse one, and the row looks ordinary afterwards.

Found by checking a teammate's live-path headroom claim, which was correct for a bare
library caller (4096/600) and did not cover the kernel's env overrides.

